# ha-rive-cards — project rules

This repo builds custom Home Assistant Lovelace cards that render Rive animations.
Stack: Lit 3, TypeScript (strict), Vite library build, `@rive-app/webgl2`.
Output: one ES module, `dist/ha-rive-cards.js`, loaded by HA as a dashboard resource and distributed through HACS.

## Layout

- `src/index.ts` — entry. Registers every card with `customElements.define` and adds it to `window.customCards`.
- `src/types.ts` — minimal HA types (`HomeAssistant`, `HassEntity`, config interfaces). Add to these rather than pulling in `custom-card-helpers`.
- `src/cards/base-rive-card.ts` — abstract base class. All Rive cards extend it.
- `src/cards/<name>-card.ts` — one file per card.
- `src/editors/<name>-card-editor.ts` — the visual editor for that card.
- `rives/source/` — `.rev` editor files. `rives/compiled/` — exported `.riv` files. Users copy `.riv` files to `<config>/www/rives/` and reference them as `/local/rives/<file>.riv`.

## Commands

- `npm run dev` — rebuilds `dist/` on every change (watch mode). Point an HA dashboard resource at the built file to test.
- `npm run build` — typecheck, then production build.
- `npm run rive:validate` — checks `.riv` headers in `rives/compiled/`.
- Node version is pinned in `.nvmrc`; run `nvm use` first.

## Writing a card

1. Create `src/cards/<name>-card.ts` with a class extending `BaseRiveCard<YourConfig>`, where `YourConfig extends BaseRiveCardConfig`.
2. Implement the three abstract members:
   - `validateConfig(config)` — throw `new Error("readable message")` for bad config. HA shows the message in the card.
   - `watchedEntities()` — every entity id the card reads. Only changes to these trigger a sync.
   - `syncToRive(hass, rive)` — read entity state from `hass.states` and write it to Rive inputs.
3. Optionally override `handleTap()` and `renderOverlay()`.
4. Add `static getConfigElement()` and `static getStubConfig(hass)` (see below).
5. Register the card in the `cards` array in `src/index.ts`. The tag name is what users write after `custom:`.

Do not override `render`, `updated`, `shouldUpdate`, `connectedCallback` or `disconnectedCallback` without calling `super` — the base class relies on them for the Rive lifecycle.

## Lit conventions

- Decorators are legacy (`experimentalDecorators: true`, `useDefineForClassFields: false`). Import from `lit/decorators.js`.
- `hass` is `@property({ attribute: false })`. Internal reactive data is `@state()`. Never reflect `hass` or config to attributes.
- Never declare a reactive property with a class field initializer (`@state() foo = 1` is fine; a plain `foo = 1` that shadows a decorated accessor is not).
- Styles go in `static styles = css\`...\``. Use HA theme variables (`--primary-color`, `--primary-text-color`, `--card-background-color`, `--error-color`) so cards follow the user's theme.
- Wrap card content in `<ha-card>`.

## Handling `hass` updates

HA sets `hass` on every card for every state change in the whole system, often several times a second. So:

- Don't re-render on `hass` changes. The base class's `shouldUpdate` returns `false` when only `hass` changed and pushes the new state into Rive instead.
- Compare entity objects by reference (`prev.states[id] !== hass.states[id]`). HA replaces the object when the entity changes, so this is cheap and exact.
- Keep `syncToRive` idempotent. It runs after load, after config changes, and after every relevant `hass` change. Setting an input to the value it already has must be harmless; the base helpers skip no-op writes.
- Missing entities are normal (renamed, unavailable). Return early; don't throw.

## Binding entity state to Rive state machine inputs

Use the helpers on `BaseRiveCard`. They look up the input by name in `config.state_machine` and silently do nothing if the input doesn't exist or has the wrong type.

- Boolean (`is_on` and similar): `this.setBooleanInput("is_on", ON_STATES.has(entity.state))`. Treat `on`, `open`, `home`, `playing`, `unlocked`, `active` as true unless the card needs otherwise.
- Number: `this.setNumberInput("brightness", value)`. Parse with `Number.parseFloat`, skip non-finite values, and convert ranges where the animation expects them (e.g. brightness 0–255 → 0–100).
- Trigger: `this.fireTrigger("changed")`. Fire only on a real transition — remember the previous state in a private field and compare. Never fire on the first sync after load.

Input names come from the `.riv` file, so make them configurable (as in `rive-entity-card`'s `bindings`) instead of hard-coding them, unless the card ships with its own animation.

Rive marks state machine inputs as deprecated in favour of data binding (view models). Inputs still work in the current runtime. If a new card uses data binding instead, add helpers for it on the base class rather than in the card.

## Rive lifecycle (handled by the base class)

- The Rive instance is created in `updated()` once the canvas exists, and recreated only when `src`, `artboard`, `state_machine` or `fit` change.
- `onLoad` calls `resizeDrawingSurfaceToCanvas()`, then the first `syncToRive`.
- A `ResizeObserver` on the canvas calls `resizeDrawingSurfaceToCanvas()` when the card is resized.
- `disconnectedCallback` disconnects the observer and calls `rive.cleanup()`, which frees the WASM memory and WebGL context. Browsers allow only ~16 WebGL contexts per page, so leaking instances breaks other cards. `connectedCallback` rebuilds the instance when HA reattaches the card.
- Callbacks check `this.rive === rive` so a slow load from an old instance can't touch a newer one.
- The Rive engine (`rive.wasm`, plus `rive_fallback.wasm` for older browsers) is served locally, not from a CDN. The `riveWasm` plugin in `vite.config.ts` copies both into `dist/`, and `src/rive-runtime.ts` points `RuntimeLoader` at them relative to the bundle's own URL. `base-rive-card.ts` imports `rive-runtime` before anything else; keep it that way, because the URLs must be set before the first Rive instance is created. The `.wasm` files must be deployed alongside `ha-rive-cards.js`.

## Visual editor — always build one

Every card must have a companion editor so it can be configured from the dashboard UI.

- In the card: `static async getConfigElement() { await import("../editors/<name>-card-editor"); return document.createElement("<name>-card-editor"); }`. The dynamic import keeps the editor out of the card's startup path.
- Also add `static getStubConfig(hass)` returning a sensible starting config, so the card works when picked from the card picker.
- The editor is a `LitElement` with `@property({ attribute: false }) hass` and a `setConfig(config)` method.
- Prefer HA's built-in `<ha-form>` with a `schema` of selectors (`entity`, `text`, `number`, `select`, `boolean`, `object`) and a `computeLabel` function. It matches HA's look and handles validation.
- On every change, dispatch `new CustomEvent("config-changed", { detail: { config }, bubbles: true, composed: true })`. Never mutate the config object you were given; create a new one.
- The editor file registers itself, guarded by `if (!customElements.get(tag))`.

## Registration

In `src/index.ts`, every card is defined once (guarded with `customElements.get`) and pushed into `window.customCards` with `type` (the tag), `name`, `description` and `preview`. This makes it appear in HA's "Add card" picker.

## Quality bar

- `npm run build` must pass with no TypeScript errors.
- No `any`. Use the types in `src/types.ts`.
- No runtime dependencies beyond `lit` and `@rive-app/webgl2` without a clear reason; everything ships in one bundle.
